# Copy to .env to override compose defaults. All optional — the stack runs as-is.

# --- host ports (change these if something else already holds one) ---
# UI_PORT=3000        the orchestrator UI          (modules' UIs are 3001…3010, fixed)
# API_PORT=9000       the orchestrator API         (modules' APIs are 9001…9010, fixed)
# MYSQL_PORT=3326     MySQL, for Workbench
#
# Only the orchestrator's two ports are variables, because they are the two that clash with
# other things in this course (a module's own compose puts its sidecar on 9000). If you need
# to move a module's port, edit docker-compose.yml — with ten of them, twenty more
# environment variables would cost more than they buy.

# --- timing ---
# STEP_DELAY=1s              pause between a status update and the next dispatch. This is the
#                            visible rhythm of the board; raise it to watch a journey walk.
# CALLBACK_TIMEOUT=30s       give up on a silent module after this. With ten steps, a journey
#                            in which every module is down takes 10 × this to fail.
# GENERATOR_INTERVAL_MS=5000 how often a new application is created while running
# GENERATOR_ENABLED=false    leave false: the stack must be up before it fires
# SIGNATURE_TIMEOUT=10m      how long a customer gets to sign the credit agreement. Its own
#                            clock, because CALLBACK_TIMEOUT is how long a MODULE may think and
#                            thirty seconds is an absurd amount of time to read a contract in.
#                            Still finite: an unbounded wait is a journey stuck for ever.

# --- demo stepping ---
# DEMO_STEPPING=false        on, nothing is dispatched until you press Proceed on the board —
#                            the FIRST step included, so eight steps is eight clicks. For
#                            narrating a journey live. Turn the generator off first, or every
#                            application it creates parks too. Usually flipped from the
#                            backoffice rather than set here; switching it back off releases
#                            everything currently held.

# --- what a module decides ---
# There are no knobs. A module's answer comes from the code its team writes in
# ApplicationService, and the skeleton's placeholder accepts everything — so an untouched
# stack completes every journey, and a journey that does NOT complete is genuinely broken
# rather than unlucky.
#
# Want a red or amber row on the board? Write a rule in a module. The generator's applicants
# vary, so one real rule gives varied outcomes that can also be EXPLAINED — which is the
# whole point, and something a probability could never do.

# --- running only some of the ten ---
# You do not need a .env for this, just name the services:
#   docker compose up orchestrator frontend-00 neo-01 frontend-01
# Steps whose module is absent time out and that application ends FAILED. That is correct
# behaviour, not a broken stack.
